Intentional removals from lib/libfetch (whole functions that nothing in
main.c or sandbox.c can reach, with their declarations; checked by linking
with --gc-sections).  When syncing, drop upstream hunks that touch them:
  fetch.c   fetchGet, fetchPut, fetchList,
            fetchGetURL, fetchXGetURL, fetchPutURL, fetchStatURL,
            fetchListURL
  file.c    fetchGetFile, fetchPutFile, fetchListFile
  ftp.c     fetchGetFTP, fetchPutFTP, fetchListFTP
  http.c    fetchGetHTTP, fetchPutHTTP, fetchListHTTP, fetchReqHTTP
  common.c  fetch_add_entry, and its "Directory-related utility functions"
            heading
  fetch.h   the declarations of the above, and struct url_ent
  common.h  the declaration of fetch_add_entry
Kept on purpose: everything inside the functions that remain, such as the
FTP STOR/APPE path, the request body arguments of http_request_body(), and
the #ifdef and #if 0 blocks.  Cutting those would make every sync a manual
port; main.c, sandbox.c and the files the port added carry the local work.

Changes to code inside functions that remain (sync them by hand):
  ftp.c     ftp_stat(): the SIZE reply is parsed with the overflow check that
            http_parse_num() in http.c uses, so a value above LLONG_MAX is a
            protocol error and reads as "size unknown", instead of wrapping
            to a negative or small size.  FreeBSD has the unchecked loop.
  http.c    http_request_body(): a chunked response ignores Content-Length, as
            RFC 9112 6.3 says, instead of cutting the decoded body off at it.
            FreeBSD stops at Content-Length and exits 0 with a short body.
  main.c    fetch(): at the end of the data the stream is closed and a failed
            close is a failed transfer.  An FTP server's final status (226,
            426, 451...) is read by that close, so when the size is unknown a
            server that aborted the transfer is no longer reported as success.
            FreeBSD ignores the status and exits 0 with a partial file.
  ftp.c     ftp_pwd(): the copy loop stops one byte short of the buffer, so
            the terminating NUL always has room; the old "pwdlen--" test let a
            server's PWD answer of PATH_MAX characters or more write the NUL
            one byte past the end.  Such an answer is now a protocol error.
  ftp.c     ftp_cwd(): the common-prefix test reads file[i] before file[i - 1],
            and the latter only when i > 0; with an empty PWD answer the old
            order read the byte before the start of the name.
  common.c  fetch_ssl(): the server name passed to libtls has the brackets of
            an IPv6 literal removed.  The URL parser keeps them in the host,
            and libtls rejected "[::1]" as a server name, so https://[::1]/
            never connected.  (libtls only; FreeBSD's OpenSSL code has the
            same bracketed host in fetch_ssl_verify_hname() and never
            verifies an IP6 SAN.)
